1
00:00:30,965 --> 00:00:33,340
Plasma Youth Sports Games
with Unsuccess of the champion,

2
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,050
would like to thank Slaven Bilić
for stepping in as the host.

3
00:00:36,155 --> 00:00:39,327
Bilić is one of the first sponsors,
ambassador and is our biggest promoter.

4
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,302
He used his life and sports experience
in order to prepare high-quality content

5
00:00:42,546 --> 00:00:45,037
with his guests for all generations.
Thanks, Nane!

6
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:52,300
Tell me, what did it feel like
to play for Serbia in the Fed Cup?

7
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:54,410
You lost the final, right?
-Yes.

8
00:00:54,525 --> 00:00:59,445
Did that hurt more than the final
in Paris? -Yes, it hurt more.

9
00:00:59,551 --> 00:01:03,975
I'm so sorry about that because we had
such a great team with Jelena, me,

10
00:01:04,106 --> 00:01:06,124
and the other girls.

11
00:01:06,293 --> 00:01:10,483
It was truly a huge achievement for
such a small country to reach the final.

12
00:01:10,827 --> 00:01:13,968
What was missing?
-Team spirit, maybe.

13
00:01:14,068 --> 00:01:15,100
Really? -Yes.

14
00:01:15,211 --> 00:01:16,571
Maybe that...

15
00:01:16,636 --> 00:01:19,358
Why, if all three of you were from
Serbia and you were friends too?

16
00:01:19,522 --> 00:01:23,882
Yes, but we didn't have that feeling of,
"All for one, one for all."

17
00:01:24,219 --> 00:01:26,483
Everyone had their own thing going on,
but there wasn't that unity

18
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:28,754
that you need in a team sport,
as you would understand.

19
00:01:28,853 --> 00:01:31,007
I do, but we're together
every single day. -Exactly.

20
00:01:31,091 --> 00:01:34,030
That's why I'm saying it.
We're together every day.

21
00:01:34,815 --> 00:01:40,272
Whether it's Manchester United,
Hajduk, or Partizan. -You're right.

22
00:01:40,562 --> 00:01:43,795
And you weren't together every day.
-No. Maybe that's the reason?

23
00:01:43,983 --> 00:01:49,023
It's the same in the Davis Cup...
-Yes, that sense of unity.

24
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:50,983
But how can you have it?
-You're right.

25
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:54,995
I don't know. Maybe that's why we all
chose an individual sport. -Yes.

26
00:01:55,244 --> 00:01:59,684
Because we're individualists.
Maybe that's what we lacked?

27
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,245
And there was something else missing.

28
00:02:02,362 --> 00:02:05,394
Back then, Krunić was already
playing great doubles,

29
00:02:05,551 --> 00:02:07,956
and she later reached the
Roland Garros doubles final,

30
00:02:08,162 --> 00:02:11,482
but at that time, we maybe
didn't have a strong doubles team.

31
00:02:11,643 --> 00:02:13,921
And in the Fed Cup, especially
when you reach the final...

32
00:02:14,015 --> 00:02:15,479
That is a deciding factor.

33
00:02:15,557 --> 00:02:18,382
Yes, because there are so few matches.
-Exactly. -Yes.

34
00:02:19,422 --> 00:02:21,062
Let's talk a little about tennis.

35
00:02:21,193 --> 00:02:27,593
You keep mentioning
the mental state of a player.

36
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,500
We're going back to that again...

37
00:02:32,334 --> 00:02:34,174
Tennis is a brutal sport.

38
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:38,829
On the one hand, it's a bit underrated,
in regards to how difficult it is,

39
00:02:38,976 --> 00:02:42,095
by people like me,
watching it as an amateur.

40
00:02:42,181 --> 00:02:45,621
It's seen as a gentleman's sport,
the "white circus".

41
00:02:45,751 --> 00:02:48,962
There's no physical contact,
so people think there are no injuries.

42
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,250
That's why people often say
it's a pleasure to play,

43
00:02:51,336 --> 00:02:52,759
meant for gentleman.

44
00:02:52,882 --> 00:02:55,969
But then, on the other hand, Agassi
says tennis players are gladiators.

45
00:02:56,240 --> 00:03:02,571
You, Goran, and especially Novak,
all emphasize mental strength.

46
00:03:02,959 --> 00:03:05,545
You say, "Everyone serves well,
everyone trains hard,

47
00:03:05,638 --> 00:03:08,238
everyone has a great forehand,
a great backhand,

48
00:03:08,387 --> 00:03:10,857
but psychological preparation
is still the most important thing."

49
00:03:11,013 --> 00:03:14,178
And you always stress that.
-It's especially important today.

50
00:03:14,287 --> 00:03:16,975
I remember when I was just starting
to play professional tournaments.

51
00:03:17,117 --> 00:03:20,280
Back then, it was almost guaranteed
that the top 16 seeds at a Grand Slam

52
00:03:20,356 --> 00:03:24,100
would reach the third or fourth round.
There was no doubt about it.

53
00:03:24,236 --> 00:03:27,516
They were all talented,
and that talent alone

54
00:03:27,631 --> 00:03:31,365
could carry them to the third
or fourth round of a Grand Slam.

55
00:03:31,511 --> 00:03:35,711
But what happened later?
Technology advanced.

56
00:03:35,891 --> 00:03:38,662
So players who maybe
weren't naturally talented

57
00:03:38,783 --> 00:03:43,553
to be as physically strong,
maybe they were quick,

58
00:03:43,759 --> 00:03:48,506
but lacked strength, could make up
for it with better racket strings,

59
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:54,060
or better rackets. So the advantage
talented players had over

60
00:03:54,182 --> 00:03:56,809
those who weren't as gifted
gradually became smaller.

61
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,957
Thanks to technology,
new strings, and new rackets.

62
00:03:59,118 --> 00:04:02,838
So what was left? Everyone trains hard.
Everyone wants the same thing.

63
00:04:03,022 --> 00:04:06,022
Mental preparation. Psychological
preparation became the key.

64
00:04:06,262 --> 00:04:09,822
In the end, everyone can play well,
but when it's 4-4, 30-30,

65
00:04:09,991 --> 00:04:11,511
that one point changes everything.

66
00:04:11,595 --> 00:04:15,188
The one who wins is the player with
confidence and the ability to stay calm.

67
00:04:15,656 --> 00:04:18,879
You've often said you
didn't have that. -I did...

68
00:04:19,309 --> 00:04:22,869
That it was a problem for you...
-I didn't have...

69
00:04:23,114 --> 00:04:30,539
I had mental strength, but I was
afraid of the crowd, of people.

70
00:04:30,648 --> 00:04:33,000
Of responsibility?
-Not really.

71
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:38,660
I actually like taking responsibility.
During matches, I often took risks.

72
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:42,300
I wasn't someone who only played it
safe.

73
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:46,740
My bigger problem was standing
in front of so many people.

74
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,820
I'm quite introverted.

75
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:52,140
I didn't mean that you didn't want
to take responsibility.

76
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:55,300
I meant that you took too much
responsibility upon yourself.

77
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:59,140
That you always felt you had to do
everything alone. -Yes, that's true.

78
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:03,700
Because if you really analyze tennis,
that's what I was saying

79
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,780
when Goran was our guest.
It's impossible.

80
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:11,260
It's as if they deliberately want to
make it harder for you. -You're right.

81
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:13,580
It's like in that Squid Game series.

82
00:05:14,832 --> 00:05:16,505
You're completely alone.

83
00:05:16,622 --> 00:05:19,255
As Pete Sampras says,
"I can't pass the ball." -Yes.

84
00:05:19,426 --> 00:05:21,146
You're on your own.

85
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:25,180
Until recently, your coach wasn't
even allowed to be near you, right?

86
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:27,540
Now they can sit courtside.

87
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:32,060
Another brutal thing is that every
other sport is played against the clock.

88
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:37,060
If you're ahead, you can slow the pace,
force your opponent to take more risks,

89
00:05:37,181 --> 00:05:38,708
and then beat them.

90
00:05:38,826 --> 00:05:41,622
In tennis, you can't do that.
You have to play until the very end.

91
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,926
Let's remember your match
against Kim Clijsters.

92
00:05:44,048 --> 00:05:47,044
The score was 5-1 and 40-0.
-That's right, 40-0.

93
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:51,505
How is it possible to lose from there?
-I don't know. I honestly don't know.

94
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:56,980
She played those few points
really well, and...

95
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,260
But tennis is a very strange sport,
and it wasn't the first time

96
00:05:59,348 --> 00:06:00,767
something like that happened.

97
00:06:00,911 --> 00:06:04,392
You remember it also happened in match
Gaudio against Coria. -Yes, I remember.

98
00:06:05,973 --> 00:06:09,549
The problem is that sometimes you're not
even thinking about the final result.

99
00:06:09,772 --> 00:06:14,293
You win one point after another,
and then victory slowly slips away.

100
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:17,180
So you don't even realize it.

101
00:06:17,453 --> 00:06:19,548
Do you think the frequent breaks
are part of the problem?

102
00:06:19,698 --> 00:06:22,228
I think they are, but I want to hear
your opinion, because you're the expert.

103
00:06:22,379 --> 00:06:24,673
Are all those breaks part of the
problem?

104
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:29,789
Every few games there's a break,
and you sit there alone for a full minute.

105
00:06:30,205 --> 00:06:33,859
If you played continuously,
you'd probably play differently.

106
00:06:34,126 --> 00:06:36,843
You'd trust your instincts more.

107
00:06:37,193 --> 00:06:41,429
Instead, every few games you have
another break, and that's enough time

108
00:06:41,624 --> 00:06:44,761
to lose your mental edge if the match
isn't going your way. -You're right.

109
00:06:45,062 --> 00:06:48,523
That's why you sometimes notice
those swings in a player's game.

110
00:06:48,825 --> 00:06:51,305
Just one point can completely
change the course of a match.

111
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:56,031
Of course, the players at the very top
know how to recognize that moment

112
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:59,460
and turn the match back in their favor.

113
00:07:00,442 --> 00:07:03,960
But it's not easy. -That's why
mental preparation is so important.

114
00:07:04,179 --> 00:07:08,468
Exactly. You're alone, traveling
and competing every single week.

115
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:12,220
For example, if you win a tournament
on Sunday, another one starts on Monday.

116
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:15,740
Nobody talks about Sunday's victory,
because you have to win again on Monday.

117
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:21,380
And in the end, after the match
and the press conference,

118
00:07:21,479 --> 00:07:24,302
you go back to your hotel,
and once again, you're completely alone.

119
00:07:24,508 --> 00:07:28,419
Most of the time, you're in
a different time zone.

120
00:07:28,545 --> 00:07:32,388
You can't even call
your friends or your family.

121
00:07:32,743 --> 00:07:36,583
It does make things
mentally more difficult.

122
00:07:36,729 --> 00:07:40,103
And then, in the end,
all your ranking points disappear.

123
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:46,940
That's why tennis players are often
accompanied by their families. You need...

124
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:50,500
Because you don't have teammates,
you don't have a locker room,

125
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:55,060
you don't have a home base,
you don't have a safe place.

126
00:07:55,197 --> 00:07:58,957
Absolutely. You spend more time with
your coach than with your own family.

127
00:07:59,063 --> 00:08:01,445
Your coach becomes the most
important person in your life.

128
00:08:01,545 --> 00:08:05,655
They're your teacher, your critic,
your shoulder to cry on and everything.

129
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:09,585
Back in your day, you didn't have
a whole support team, right?

130
00:08:09,686 --> 00:08:11,639
There were no sports psychologists
or anything like that.

131
00:08:11,773 --> 00:08:15,420
How big was your team? -It depended.
At first, it was one or two people.

132
00:08:15,559 --> 00:08:19,397
Later I had a physiotherapist,
a coach, and a hitting partner.

133
00:08:19,493 --> 00:08:21,493
Eventually there were
three or four of us.

134
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:23,772
It wasn't like the large teams
players have today.

135
00:08:23,885 --> 00:08:25,639
So the coach is extremely important.

136
00:08:25,758 --> 00:08:27,678
You changed coaches
quite often, didn't you?

137
00:08:27,856 --> 00:08:30,561
What were you looking for
that you couldn't find?

138
00:08:31,295 --> 00:08:35,855
I think that's something
people underestimate.

139
00:08:36,016 --> 00:08:38,694
People often say that women are
always changing coaches.

140
00:08:38,835 --> 00:08:44,155
For a long time, I worked with Sven,
who worked for Adidas.

141
00:08:44,387 --> 00:08:50,639
For several years, Adidas ran a program
with two coaches who looked

142
00:08:50,766 --> 00:08:53,726
after their sponsored players.

143
00:08:53,928 --> 00:08:56,448
I had a great relationship with Sven.

144
00:08:56,648 --> 00:09:00,648
At one point, I wanted to hire him
as my full-time personal coach.

145
00:09:00,850 --> 00:09:04,250
But two days before the Australian Open,
he told me he didn't want to do it

146
00:09:04,356 --> 00:09:06,196
and left me on my own.

147
00:09:06,372 --> 00:09:10,780
I was suddenly without a coach.
And it happened twice.

148
00:09:10,940 --> 00:09:15,620
Once before the Australian Open,
and once two years earlier,

149
00:09:15,735 --> 00:09:18,215
when he simply came to me
and said he didn't want

150
00:09:18,337 --> 00:09:20,376
to coach me anymore.

151
00:09:20,464 --> 00:09:23,569
Why? -Without any explanation.
-Were you difficult to work with?

152
00:09:23,762 --> 00:09:25,655
I don't know. Maybe I was.

153
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:29,545
I honestly don't know,
but I remember it vividly.

154
00:09:29,621 --> 00:09:31,624
It was very hard for me to get over it.

155
00:09:31,764 --> 00:09:33,721
Because it happened at
a very difficult time in my life.

156
00:09:33,841 --> 00:09:36,827
The first time was in 2009,
and then again in 2011.

157
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:42,820
After that, you try to find
someone who really suits you,

158
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:46,100
because you spend
so much time together.

159
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:49,060
You can't keep changing coaches.

160
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:52,540
And coaches also like to have
everything under their control,

161
00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:55,660
which often leads to conflicts.

162
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:59,180
I'm not someone
who likes going out,

163
00:09:59,333 --> 00:10:01,205
so my coach didn't have
to send me to my room.

164
00:10:01,295 --> 00:10:02,935
I'm not like that.

165
00:10:03,002 --> 00:10:05,487
But I do enjoy going for a walk,
shopping, going to the beach,

166
00:10:05,592 --> 00:10:07,275
or just doing something else.

167
00:10:07,392 --> 00:10:10,544
None of that was allowed.
I was expected to sit in my room.

168
00:10:10,913 --> 00:10:14,974
After a while, that starts to
bother you. -You're all alone.

169
00:10:15,105 --> 00:10:18,130
When you look at an entire support
staff, it's the same in football.

170
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:22,980
A coaching staff can have twenty people.
Nowadays, they often have twenty or thirty.

171
00:10:23,312 --> 00:10:25,325
But you still have teammates.

172
00:10:25,488 --> 00:10:27,968
There will always be a group
of players who want to bend

173
00:10:28,106 --> 00:10:29,529
the rules a little.

174
00:10:29,595 --> 00:10:32,754
Train a little less... So they can
spend some time at the beach.

175
00:10:32,811 --> 00:10:34,851
But here, you're all by yourself.

176
00:10:34,988 --> 00:10:38,309
Yes. -You're the only one who
wants a little freedom...

177
00:10:38,441 --> 00:10:41,809
And then it's easy to fall under
their influence. -Exactly.

178
00:10:42,041 --> 00:10:44,450
And that's exactly
what I wanted to ask you.

179
00:10:44,589 --> 00:10:47,834
I watched a podcast, one of
the very few you have appeared on.

180
00:10:48,004 --> 00:10:53,287
There were a female and a male host,
I mean, it was a tennis podcast,

181
00:10:54,107 --> 00:11:01,587
and the male host said that the vast
majority of players, once they retire,

182
00:11:01,952 --> 00:11:08,143
are left with the same thought:
"I wish I had enjoyed it more."

183
00:11:10,119 --> 00:11:13,057
And you immediately jumped in
and said, "Absolutely. That’s exactly it."

184
00:11:13,251 --> 00:11:15,663
One hundred percent.
-Was that true for you as well?

185
00:11:15,845 --> 00:11:23,236
Yes, it was. I remember that moment very
clearly, at the 2016 US Open.

186
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:26,100
I already knew it would be
my final US Open,

187
00:11:26,332 --> 00:11:29,972
although no one else knew
apart from my best friend, Kerber.

188
00:11:30,079 --> 00:11:31,439
The two of us went out for coffee.

189
00:11:31,515 --> 00:11:34,675
She went on to win the US Open
that year, so she was at her peak,

190
00:11:34,774 --> 00:11:37,534
while I was... It was
extremely difficult for me,

191
00:11:37,658 --> 00:11:39,818
and I remember
talking to her about it.

192
00:11:40,374 --> 00:11:44,134
You become so deeply absorbed
in that entire world

193
00:11:44,321 --> 00:11:47,192
and begin to feel the full weight of it.
Because it is difficult.

194
00:11:47,365 --> 00:11:49,489
First, you have to say goodbye
to a sport you love,

195
00:11:49,675 --> 00:11:54,115
one that has been your entire life.
And secondly, you are caught inside

196
00:11:54,268 --> 00:11:58,748
this machine of constant
criticism and endless opinions.

197
00:12:01,270 --> 00:12:04,470
People always have something to say.
I keep coming back to that.

198
00:12:04,631 --> 00:12:08,791
I do not know why I spent so much time
on thinking about those things

199
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,620
instead of turning my attention inward
and focusing more on myself.

200
00:12:12,732 --> 00:12:16,620
Because, in the end, everything
comes and goes. My career ended.

201
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:19,488
No one talks about it anymore. Now they
are talking about someone else.

202
00:12:19,599 --> 00:12:21,479
The moment you retire,
that chapter is over.

203
00:12:21,586 --> 00:12:23,666
And very few people know that
while it is actually happening.

204
00:12:23,812 --> 00:12:27,492
No one knows. I remember
telling her that my only regret was

205
00:12:27,606 --> 00:12:29,526
that I had not enjoyed it more.

206
00:12:31,402 --> 00:12:34,066
But at the same time, it is
difficult to enjoy yourself when

207
00:12:34,183 --> 00:12:38,283
you are not achieving the results
you wanted and trained so hard for.

208
00:12:38,789 --> 00:12:41,769
That is one hundred percent true.

209
00:12:42,403 --> 00:12:46,027
Do you think you could have achieved the
same, or something close to it,

210
00:12:46,195 --> 00:12:48,167
with slightly fewer sacrifices?

211
00:12:48,401 --> 00:12:51,113
Is it actually possible to find that
balance everyone is searching for?

212
00:12:51,270 --> 00:12:56,308
I think what ultimately shaped
a great deal of my career

213
00:12:56,534 --> 00:13:01,855
was winning Roland Garros. Up until that
point, as we have already said,

214
00:13:02,001 --> 00:13:04,634
it had been one success after another.

215
00:13:04,805 --> 00:13:08,415
Of course, there had been defeats
as well, but I was young,

216
00:13:08,593 --> 00:13:11,233
and somehow everything still felt like
part of that natural progression.

217
00:13:11,512 --> 00:13:15,672
The year I won Roland Garros,
I remember my coach telling me

218
00:13:15,858 --> 00:13:20,938
before every match, my parents and
my brother were in Paris that year,

219
00:13:21,039 --> 00:13:24,339
"Ana, you are not allowed to have dinner
with your parents because

220
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:26,612
it takes too much energy out of you.

221
00:13:26,786 --> 00:13:30,197
You may go to dinner at seven with your
brother and your fitness coach,

222
00:13:30,313 --> 00:13:33,112
and that is it. Afterwards,
you go straight back to your room."

223
00:13:33,274 --> 00:13:36,832
I remember, every evening,
my brother Miloš and I would go

224
00:13:36,968 --> 00:13:38,688
to dinner with my fitness coach.

225
00:13:38,866 --> 00:13:41,160
We went to the same restaurant
near the hotel every day,

226
00:13:41,294 --> 00:13:43,879
ate the same kind of meal, and then went
straight back to the room.

227
00:13:44,034 --> 00:13:46,418
My brother and I were
sharing a room at the time.

228
00:13:46,512 --> 00:13:49,340
By nine o’clock, we would be sitting
there wondering what to do.

229
00:13:49,509 --> 00:13:53,277
I would read, he would watch TV,
or we would both read and talk.

230
00:13:53,738 --> 00:13:57,353
The problem, although perhaps "problem"
is not the right word,

231
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:00,580
was that it worked.
I won the tournament.

232
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:04,700
So in my mind, sacrifice became directly
associated with success.

233
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:09,420
Later in my career, even when I wanted
to relax a little or go somewhere,

234
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:11,830
and I am only talking about shopping or
spending a day at the beach,

235
00:14:11,905 --> 00:14:13,545
I felt I was not allowed to do it.

236
00:14:13,644 --> 00:14:16,542
I would think, "This will come back
to punish me. I will be tired.

237
00:14:16,635 --> 00:14:18,555
I will not be able to play
or train properly."

238
00:14:18,679 --> 00:14:21,322
So it became one sacrifice after
another. -I understand.

239
00:14:21,626 --> 00:14:25,525
And I am not talking about holidays
or anything extravagant.

240
00:14:25,714 --> 00:14:28,376
I mean just a few hours
or a couple of days.

241
00:14:29,540 --> 00:14:32,260
It is a shame, because as you say,
it all comes to an end.

242
00:14:32,477 --> 00:14:34,733
Perhaps there was a way
to do things differently,

243
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:37,858
although finding that balance
is incredibly difficult.

244
00:14:38,064 --> 00:14:40,344
Apart from that,
what else would you change?

245
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:44,037
Is there one match you would
bring back and play differently?

246
00:14:46,540 --> 00:14:49,700
The Australian Open final
against Sharapova.

247
00:14:49,823 --> 00:14:53,143
I would love to be able to go back
and play that match again.

248
00:14:53,633 --> 00:14:56,113
But what I would
most like to change is...

249
00:14:56,297 --> 00:14:58,377
Your relationship with everything
that came afterwards?

250
00:14:58,563 --> 00:15:04,170
The way I dealt with everything
after the injury in 2008.

251
00:15:04,478 --> 00:15:07,412
When I had to withdraw from the
Olympics, it affected me so deeply,

252
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:10,500
and somehow I never
fully recovered from it.

253
00:15:10,598 --> 00:15:13,045
For several months,
I went onto the court every day

254
00:15:13,138 --> 00:15:16,794
hoping I would be able to play,
and every day I was in pain.

255
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:20,623
At the same time, I was doing fitness
work so I could maintain my form.

256
00:15:20,813 --> 00:15:24,389
I did not really have
the support I needed.

257
00:15:24,479 --> 00:15:26,873
Perhaps Sven did not
help me enough in that respect.

258
00:15:27,012 --> 00:15:28,917
He was there, but he did not
truly help me through it.

259
00:15:29,014 --> 00:15:30,974
Maybe if I had had
a psychologist at the time,

260
00:15:31,093 --> 00:15:33,326
someone who could have advised me
on how to process everything,

261
00:15:33,427 --> 00:15:34,907
it would have been different.

262
00:15:35,028 --> 00:15:37,074
Instead, I went to the US Open
completely unprepared.

263
00:15:37,174 --> 00:15:39,644
I lost in the second round
as the number-one seed.

264
00:15:39,682 --> 00:15:41,722
I was nowhere near ready
for that tournament.

265
00:15:41,814 --> 00:15:44,363
I had trained for only a week,
perhaps ten days, beforehand.

266
00:15:44,486 --> 00:15:46,006
Yes. It is such a shame.

267
00:15:46,222 --> 00:15:50,269
And that thought
"I wish I had enjoyed it more"

268
00:15:50,561 --> 00:15:54,881
is probably connected
more strongly to individual sports.

269
00:15:55,567 --> 00:16:00,487
In tennis, of course, there are players
who did not give their all,

270
00:16:00,688 --> 00:16:03,728
and then regret it after their careers
because they know they could have.

271
00:16:03,897 --> 00:16:06,337
But that happens far more often
in team sports.

272
00:16:06,512 --> 00:16:08,392
You hear many more athletes say,

273
00:16:08,625 --> 00:16:11,386
"I should have dedicated myself
more seriously to my career."

274
00:16:11,519 --> 00:16:14,425
Because in a team sport,
someone else can carry you.

275
00:16:14,587 --> 00:16:16,738
You can afford to have a poor season,

276
00:16:16,905 --> 00:16:19,824
because your teammates
may pull you through.

277
00:16:20,123 --> 00:16:23,523
But in tennis, there is no one
to do that for you.

278
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:25,821
Everything rests entirely
on your shoulders.

279
00:16:26,007 --> 00:16:28,313
And then you have a staff
constantly putting demands on you.

280
00:16:28,397 --> 00:16:30,196
Today, those teams are
becoming larger and larger,

281
00:16:30,313 --> 00:16:32,153
and everyone wants their own
share of your time and attention.

282
00:16:32,213 --> 00:16:34,995
There is a nutritionist, a massage
therapist, a physiotherapist,

283
00:16:35,074 --> 00:16:37,034
a doctor, your main coach,
a separate serving coach,

284
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:39,209
and everyone else around you.

285
00:16:39,311 --> 00:16:42,366
And ultimately, you are the only person
who can stand up and say,

286
00:16:42,515 --> 00:16:46,577
"Wait a moment, I have a life as well. I
would like to live a little."

287
00:16:46,794 --> 00:16:49,359
Unfortunately,
that is very difficult to do.

288
00:16:49,807 --> 00:16:53,687
I remember one coach
I had at Roland Garros.

289
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:59,360
I lost a match, I cannot remember
who I played or even what year it was,

290
00:16:59,474 --> 00:17:01,813
and afterwards I said to him,
"Everything you told me

291
00:17:01,916 --> 00:17:04,516
about this player,
the entire tactical plan, was wrong."

292
00:17:04,592 --> 00:17:07,952
And he replied, "That is because
I was watching a different player."

293
00:17:08,034 --> 00:17:09,874
They were both Russian
and had similar surnames,

294
00:17:09,972 --> 00:17:12,563
so he had given me
a tactical plan for someone else.

295
00:17:12,627 --> 00:17:15,898
I said, "Everything was wrong, and now I
have to go into a press conference.

296
00:17:16,086 --> 00:17:19,492
What am I supposed to say, that my coach
gave me the wrong tactics?"

297
00:17:19,655 --> 00:17:23,055
People would think I was completely
unprofessional. What could I do?

298
00:17:23,131 --> 00:17:24,797
I had to take the blame,

299
00:17:24,836 --> 00:17:26,985
even though he was the one
who had prepared me for the match.

300
00:17:27,099 --> 00:17:29,259
I think that is one of the major
differences in an individual sport.

301
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:30,430
Yes, exactly.

302
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:33,688
And then there are mornings
when you wake up and something hurts.

303
00:17:33,835 --> 00:17:38,305
In 2014, I think I spent
six months taking Voltaren.

304
00:17:38,384 --> 00:17:41,224
I could barely get out of bed. I could
not even put my socks on properly,

305
00:17:41,362 --> 00:17:44,047
I had to pull them on awkwardly because
everything hurt.

306
00:17:44,261 --> 00:17:46,581
I had problems with my back
and a trapped nerve,

307
00:17:46,751 --> 00:17:50,781
yet I won three tournaments that year.
-That is the price you pay.

308
00:17:51,034 --> 00:17:55,754
Exactly. That is why people say it is
brutal. -It is brutal. It truly is.

309
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:02,060
You and Novak were born
in the same year, correct? -Yes.

310
00:18:04,980 --> 00:18:09,180
Men's and women's tennis are effectively
separate worlds,

311
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:12,700
but what is your relationship like?
-We grew up together.

312
00:18:12,789 --> 00:18:15,309
We have known each other
since we were four years old.

313
00:18:15,446 --> 00:18:17,446
He trained with Pilić, of course,

314
00:18:17,585 --> 00:18:19,465
but he often came back to Serbia
for tournaments.

315
00:18:19,611 --> 00:18:24,117
So even when we were 10, 12 or 14,

316
00:18:24,358 --> 00:18:26,518
we were already playing
at many of the same tournaments.

317
00:18:26,758 --> 00:18:31,958
Then we moved into professional tennis
at almost exactly the same time.

318
00:18:32,464 --> 00:18:35,184
During those first few years,
we were inseparable.

319
00:18:35,329 --> 00:18:37,069
We were constantly
going out for dinner,

320
00:18:37,238 --> 00:18:40,638
and we played the Hopman Cup
together several times. So...

321
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:43,780
What do you admire most about him?

322
00:18:43,878 --> 00:18:45,838
Definitely the mental side
of his game.

323
00:18:45,959 --> 00:18:50,887
I don't think anyone has ever mastered
that aspect of sport the way he has.

324
00:18:51,010 --> 00:18:54,629
The way he has? -Yes. And the fact that
he continues to push himself

325
00:18:54,768 --> 00:18:57,754
and still wants more after
everything he has already achieved.

326
00:18:57,877 --> 00:19:00,793
I am not sure people fully understand
what he has accomplished,

327
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:05,420
how extraordinary
and almost unreal it is.

328
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:08,980
I honestly do not believe
anyone will ever surpass it.

329
00:19:09,540 --> 00:19:14,228
You are the same age,
yet he is still playing.

330
00:19:14,376 --> 00:19:16,923
He is still reaching
Grand Slam finals,

331
00:19:17,145 --> 00:19:22,134
while you have already
been retired for almost ten years.

332
00:19:24,248 --> 00:19:29,688
Is that connected to motherhood?
Does the desire to become a mother

333
00:19:29,973 --> 00:19:34,517
place a time limit on a woman's career,
not only for athletes,

334
00:19:34,636 --> 00:19:38,142
but for women in business
and other professions as well?

335
00:19:38,289 --> 00:19:41,235
Absolutely.
I am convinced that it does.

336
00:19:41,321 --> 00:19:43,121
Even players today talk about it,

337
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:46,632
although some of them are now returning
after having children.

338
00:19:46,786 --> 00:19:49,889
Yes. -They return to the tour
after becoming mothers.

339
00:19:49,914 --> 00:19:51,846
But look at Roger, for example.

340
00:19:51,919 --> 00:19:54,467
He was able to have children in his late
twenties or early thirties

341
00:19:54,590 --> 00:19:56,455
and continue playing.
Novak did the same.

342
00:19:56,554 --> 00:19:58,319
His children are actually
older than mine.

343
00:19:58,458 --> 00:20:01,378
It is very different
when you are a woman.

344
00:20:01,493 --> 00:20:03,666
Yes, there is a major difference.

345
00:20:03,814 --> 00:20:08,823
Men can continue playing without
carrying the same physical burden.

346
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:12,737
Returning to tennis after having
children was never my dream.

347
00:20:12,832 --> 00:20:18,846
I felt fortunate that my career had
happened while I was still young,

348
00:20:18,944 --> 00:20:21,504
and I had always wanted to devote myself
fully to my children.

349
00:20:21,610 --> 00:20:24,290
I didn't want to start travelling
and spending time away from them.

350
00:20:24,522 --> 00:20:27,642
But women definitely
experience more pressure.

351
00:20:27,757 --> 00:20:31,612
And it is not only pressure,
they also carry guilt later.

352
00:20:31,797 --> 00:20:35,717
That applies to mothers not only
in sport, but in every profession.

353
00:20:35,887 --> 00:20:38,247
The moment you have to
do something for yourself,

354
00:20:38,439 --> 00:20:41,732
or leave your child at nursery or school
so that you can go to work,

355
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:45,420
that guilt appears.
And it never truly goes away.

356
00:20:45,648 --> 00:20:48,793
Yes. The problem is...
But you were also fortunate.

357
00:20:48,969 --> 00:20:51,613
You had the talent and the quality,

358
00:20:51,733 --> 00:20:55,121
and circumstances came together
in a way that allowed you to fulfil

359
00:20:55,257 --> 00:21:00,337
your dreams very early,
both professionally and financially.

360
00:21:00,623 --> 00:21:03,918
You were able to say, "I am satisfied. I
can walk away now." -Yes.

361
00:21:04,138 --> 00:21:09,018
But when a woman has not yet
achieved the results she wanted,

362
00:21:09,202 --> 00:21:12,199
whether in sport,
business or any other career,

363
00:21:12,299 --> 00:21:15,127
she still wants to continue.
Then frustration begins to build,

364
00:21:15,245 --> 00:21:17,464
while at the same time she feels
that time is running out.

365
00:21:17,570 --> 00:21:20,682
That must be extremely difficult.
-It is. Absolutely. I agree.

366
00:21:21,213 --> 00:21:24,333
I was truly fortunate that so many
things came together for me,

367
00:21:24,486 --> 00:21:27,292
because success is incredibly complex.
So many elements are required,

368
00:21:27,491 --> 00:21:30,211
not only talent,
but hard work, sacrifice,

369
00:21:30,426 --> 00:21:33,226
support and financial resources.
Everything.

370
00:21:33,834 --> 00:21:36,554
And even when you have all of that,

371
00:21:36,777 --> 00:21:39,857
you still have to turn it
into actual results.

372
00:21:40,327 --> 00:21:43,287
Yes, yes. I completely agree.

373
00:21:44,474 --> 00:21:47,417
You have also said, and I do not
particularly like dividing sport

374
00:21:47,517 --> 00:21:49,684
into "men's" and "women's"
sport either,

375
00:21:49,856 --> 00:21:54,936
that those divisions are usually
based on physical differences.

376
00:21:55,114 --> 00:21:58,481
People assume men are more dominant,
stronger and so on.

377
00:21:58,939 --> 00:22:03,965
But you are saying that the male and
female mind work differently.

378
00:22:04,104 --> 00:22:06,224
They simply function differently.

379
00:22:06,393 --> 00:22:09,553
That for women it's emotionally
much harder than for men.

380
00:22:09,737 --> 00:22:13,217
I believe that is so.
-Why? -I believe that is so.

381
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:17,820
I think women react much more
emotionally than men.

382
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:23,540
The mere fact that hormones exist,
monthly cycles in women and all that,

383
00:22:23,780 --> 00:22:27,700
that already affects
a person's emotions.

384
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:31,060
I think it affects it much more
in women than in men.

385
00:22:31,711 --> 00:22:34,111
And that men handle it differently.

386
00:22:34,327 --> 00:22:36,527
They simply have a different mindset.

387
00:22:36,706 --> 00:22:39,586
A lot of male players
travel with friends.

388
00:22:39,842 --> 00:22:41,442
To them, that's normal.

389
00:22:41,735 --> 00:22:45,500
They joke around if things get hard,
go out to dinner, and that's it.

390
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:48,060
With women, it's not like that.

391
00:22:48,340 --> 00:22:52,140
If women find it hard,
they'll gather aorund, talk, cry...

392
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:55,140
It's different. Of course...

393
00:22:56,100 --> 00:23:00,100
I don't know, that's why
I think quite a few women

394
00:23:00,260 --> 00:23:06,921
in sports have a much harder time
maintaining that level than men do.

395
00:23:07,220 --> 00:23:11,180
We have Serena, who won
so many grand slams.

396
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:16,860
Why in that podcast, where the three
of you, you and the two hosts,

397
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:20,420
do you say that essentially
a female tennis player

398
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:26,420
has it easier if she is
in a relationship?

399
00:23:26,660 --> 00:23:30,700
That she needs a partner?
Does that give you stability? -Yes.

400
00:23:30,900 --> 00:23:34,540
But, that is also something
that is not really acceptable.

401
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:38,380
I remember, and all the girls
on the tour can confirm it.

402
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:41,180
If a girl has a boyfriend,
it is considered that she is no longer

403
00:23:41,420 --> 00:23:43,740
focused on the sport.
If a man has a girlfriend,

404
00:23:43,940 --> 00:23:46,580
it is considered that he is
stable and will play better.

405
00:23:46,780 --> 00:23:48,700
That was an unwritten rule.

406
00:23:48,900 --> 00:23:52,060
That's how we grew up.
Now that is changing.

407
00:23:52,220 --> 00:23:54,740
Sabalenka travels with her fiancé.

408
00:23:54,940 --> 00:23:59,100
And Cibulkova traveled too. That was
already toward the end of my career.

409
00:23:59,420 --> 00:24:04,860
It's not as frequent
as it was the case for men.

410
00:24:05,340 --> 00:24:09,180
A large number of male players
are already married.

411
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:12,500
With female players,
that is not present. -Yes, yes.

412
00:24:13,885 --> 00:24:15,445
Interesting.

413
00:24:15,817 --> 00:24:17,737
Tell me this detail.

414
00:24:18,473 --> 00:24:23,473
It's no secret, you're beautiful,
attractive, have that smile, charm...

415
00:24:23,595 --> 00:24:29,448
Thank you. -Was that both an advantage
and a drawback for your career?

416
00:24:29,565 --> 00:24:37,682
People ask me that sometimes.
I don't see how that can be a drawback.

417
00:24:37,892 --> 00:24:41,012
By drawback I mean jealousy.
-Jealousy. -Did you feel jealousy?

418
00:24:41,135 --> 00:24:45,775
Even to this day I do. -Of course.
-Sometimes I face it.

419
00:24:45,974 --> 00:24:52,065
I believe that what I carry inside me
is what's most important.

420
00:24:52,470 --> 00:24:57,128
I don't pay much attention
to the outside world.

421
00:24:57,252 --> 00:25:00,582
I've learned that a lot
in recent years.

422
00:25:00,719 --> 00:25:04,394
Especially when you have kids,
you realize that you're no longer

423
00:25:04,566 --> 00:25:07,840
the most important.
You look at yourself differently too.

424
00:25:08,104 --> 00:25:13,140
I've learned to live with myself
in peace and that what I carry

425
00:25:13,397 --> 00:25:18,968
in my heart is my truth and who I am.
Some people like you, some don't,

426
00:25:19,179 --> 00:25:25,100
but it is what it is. Better to know
what's real, right? -For sure.

427
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:31,260
Was it for you, for instance, for you,
Sabatini, Kournikova, Sharapova...

428
00:25:32,020 --> 00:25:37,780
It can be a drawback, not just because
of jealousy, but also because of...

429
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:44,460
Like Brad Pitt in acting. It's hard
for him to get an Oscar or recognition

430
00:25:44,582 --> 00:25:47,942
like someone who isn't attractive,
because he's got this,

431
00:25:48,080 --> 00:25:50,080
because he's good-looking and handsome.

432
00:25:50,241 --> 00:25:52,081
Even though sports are exact.
-Absolutely.

433
00:25:52,194 --> 00:25:55,114
You have a result or you don't.
-... because it's because of this. -Yes.

434
00:25:55,940 --> 00:26:01,100
What was hard for me was that
at one point they were saying just that.

435
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:06,180
She just wants to take photos, to be
a model, she doesn't want to train.

436
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:09,420
That's what hurt me and
what was very hard for me,

437
00:26:09,620 --> 00:26:13,380
because it was so far from the truth.
-You essentially gave everything. -Yes.

438
00:26:13,580 --> 00:26:16,980
I even turned down many things
I was invited to,

439
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:22,460
because that didn't appeal to me
at the time. I wanted to play sports.

440
00:26:22,645 --> 00:26:24,685
To be who I am in sports.

441
00:26:25,020 --> 00:26:30,024
From that perspective,
it was a complicating factor.

442
00:26:30,580 --> 00:26:38,460
Bravo. When you compare tennis
in your time and this modern one,

443
00:26:38,700 --> 00:26:40,740
you say that it has changed a lot.

444
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:43,380
For the better, for the worse?
In what sense?

445
00:26:43,580 --> 00:26:50,580
At the time when I was playing
there was much more rivalry.

446
00:26:50,820 --> 00:26:55,380
We had both Serena and Venus,
and Sharapova, and Jelena, and me,

447
00:26:55,580 --> 00:27:00,300
and Wozniacki, and Kuznetsova,
Dementieva, Schnyder...

448
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:04,420
There were so many players
who were at the very top,

449
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:09,780
those who were competition to one
another. Currently that doesn't exist.

450
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:14,620
Every tournament we see new faces
and new champions.

451
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:17,580
That is what I miss
in today's women's tennis.

452
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:24,940
That rivalry. There's Andreeva, Marta,
they played the final in Madrid,

453
00:27:25,180 --> 00:27:29,220
then the semifinal of Roland Garros.
Sabalenka and Iga.

454
00:27:29,420 --> 00:27:32,340
There is no depth that
existed before.

455
00:27:32,714 --> 00:27:36,167
On the other hand, much more
money is in the sport.

456
00:27:37,300 --> 00:27:41,100
Tennis is becoming somehow faster.
Sabalenka is...

457
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:45,340
I like what she did with the
sport. She kind of changed

458
00:27:45,580 --> 00:27:52,460
that mentality we were talking about.
She's a personality. -She's different.

459
00:27:52,865 --> 00:28:00,154
I think that helps tennis much more
than some players who are at the top.

460
00:28:00,540 --> 00:28:04,700
You think there's no rivalry now
because top quality is missing,

461
00:28:04,862 --> 00:28:07,762
or there are too many...
-No, not too many.

462
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:09,692
It's never too many.
-I think it's missing.

463
00:28:09,961 --> 00:28:12,996
I think it's missing
at that level in consistency.

464
00:28:13,233 --> 00:28:18,301
There are players who play great
for half a year, a year, or clay season,

465
00:28:18,475 --> 00:28:20,973
hard court season and that's it.
They disappear.

466
00:28:21,128 --> 00:28:23,488
Or one Grand Slam,
quarterfinal and disappear.

467
00:28:23,587 --> 00:28:25,787
I think it's missing that consistency.

468
00:28:25,885 --> 00:28:31,489
When I talk about past generation,
Kuznetsova was also there.

469
00:28:31,751 --> 00:28:35,871
She won Roland Garros.
Serena, Venus, I'm talking about players

470
00:28:36,142 --> 00:28:40,782
who have a lot of Grand Slams.
We have Raducanu, Ostapenko,

471
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:45,891
there are many female players now
who have won one Grand Slam

472
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:49,940
and then were no longer
even in the quarterfinals. -Yes.

473
00:28:51,892 --> 00:29:00,870
You mentioned earlier... Janica says
she doesn't know if she could nowadays,

474
00:29:01,042 --> 00:29:04,540
with these social networks
and this way of life...

475
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:10,380
She doesn't know if she'd succeed.
What do you think for yourself?

476
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:16,260
Today a person is their own paparazzo.

477
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:20,780
You film yourself and if you
don't post, you don't exist.

478
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:27,980
Do you think you could do that?
-One good thing would be that

479
00:29:28,071 --> 00:29:33,911
I think I'd have many more contracts.
From that side it'd be better.

480
00:29:36,620 --> 00:29:41,380
Definitely that it adds some new
weight. And pressure to share your life.

481
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:49,300
Like I said, I love company,
hanging out, but I'm quite private.

482
00:29:49,500 --> 00:29:55,580
I don't like sharing my life with
anyone. Nowadays with Instagram,

483
00:29:55,940 --> 00:30:00,540
like you said, if you don't post,
you don't exist. -That's right.

484
00:30:00,740 --> 00:30:02,980
People take everything for granted.

485
00:30:03,180 --> 00:30:06,839
They aren't aware,
actually they probably are,

486
00:30:06,995 --> 00:30:10,214
that we can change the narrative
however we want.

487
00:30:10,403 --> 00:30:13,243
I think that's not right either.
People present themselves differently

488
00:30:13,364 --> 00:30:15,644
than what they are.
They live fake lives.

489
00:30:15,806 --> 00:30:18,366
They don't live the essence,
but present themselves differently,

490
00:30:18,606 --> 00:30:23,151
and I believe that
would also make it quite hard for me.

491
00:30:23,282 --> 00:30:30,202
If you have to post something,
you can't help seeing a comment.

492
00:30:30,660 --> 00:30:37,060
You can't help seeing what someone
retweeted or posted about you.

493
00:30:37,860 --> 00:30:41,660
If you don't post, you don't exist.
-Yes. -On the other hand,

494
00:30:41,900 --> 00:30:48,660
quality isn't the only measure. Image
was important before too. And looks.

495
00:30:48,900 --> 00:30:55,704
That did help, but today you
can reach success,

496
00:30:56,017 --> 00:30:59,485
not just in sports,
but we're talking about sports.

497
00:30:59,717 --> 00:31:03,357
Not based only on quality.
Unless you're top, top tier,

498
00:31:03,565 --> 00:31:07,245
quality isn't enough for you. -No.
-You must have this other side

499
00:31:07,405 --> 00:31:13,045
which is completely opposite, which
shifts your focus from training,

500
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:18,660
because those are totally different
things, and easier to accept. -Yes.

501
00:31:18,860 --> 00:31:24,380
Everything is narrative. Everything is
marketing. People can say on Instagram

502
00:31:24,580 --> 00:31:30,060
whatever they want. That story doesn't
have to be true. That's the problem.

503
00:31:30,300 --> 00:31:33,740
When everything is stripped down,
in the end, what is the truth.

504
00:31:33,980 --> 00:31:37,020
But, then again,
we're talking about success,

505
00:31:37,220 --> 00:31:39,740
about something you achieve in life.

506
00:31:39,980 --> 00:31:43,780
I was lucky that it was a sport
that is measured by results.

507
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:46,340
You have a result
or you don't, and that's it.

508
00:31:46,540 --> 00:31:50,300
Nowadays people live
for something external.

509
00:31:50,500 --> 00:31:53,220
Nobody pays attention to the most
important thing in life,

510
00:31:53,420 --> 00:31:57,260
and that is what you
carry inside yourself.

511
00:31:57,460 --> 00:31:59,260
That is what stays with you
for your whole life.

512
00:31:59,460 --> 00:32:01,820
Nobody works on themselves.

513
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:04,220
Everyone works on others
and everyone judges others,

514
00:32:04,460 --> 00:32:07,500
and nobody works on their own problems
when pointing fingers at others.

515
00:32:07,700 --> 00:32:10,740
What about them?
That is what's hardest for me.

516
00:32:10,940 --> 00:32:13,780
It's not hardest
because I don't engage with it,

517
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:17,100
but it's hard that people don't want

518
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:21,860
to look at themselves and fix
themselves to fix the world around them.

519
00:32:22,060 --> 00:32:26,300
Bravo. That is that machinery
that drags you in,

520
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:31,060
that chews you up, spits you out.
In a given moment it suits you,

521
00:32:31,260 --> 00:32:35,060
because you get finances, you get
popularity, you get attention.

522
00:32:35,220 --> 00:32:37,420
It's nice, right? Of course it is.

523
00:32:37,980 --> 00:32:40,140
Basically, in the long run
it's not good.

524
00:32:40,340 --> 00:32:46,580
Now we come to that topic when
champions before had to be

525
00:32:47,820 --> 00:32:51,820
mental monsters, and you say
that the phycological state,

526
00:32:51,980 --> 00:32:54,660
and I agree with that,
is the main reason for success

527
00:32:54,820 --> 00:32:57,660
and the main reason for failure.

528
00:32:57,900 --> 00:33:02,540
However, nowadays
more and more athletes are appearing

529
00:33:02,740 --> 00:33:06,380
who speak openly about their problems.

530
00:33:06,580 --> 00:33:12,340
The two sports where this is most
present are tennis and NBA basketball.

531
00:33:13,700 --> 00:33:17,940
You mentioned Sabalenka,
who has spoken about her anxiety,

532
00:33:18,260 --> 00:33:21,140
about the weight of expectations,
the pressure,

533
00:33:21,340 --> 00:33:24,780
and everything that comes with it.
We also have Michael Phelps, right?

534
00:33:24,980 --> 00:33:29,060
And then there's the incredible gymnast
Simone Biles, who essentially withdrew

535
00:33:29,660 --> 00:33:33,580
from the Tokyo Olympics before
making an amazing comeback in Paris.

536
00:33:33,820 --> 00:33:39,460
People simply aren't afraid
to talk about their problems anymore,

537
00:33:39,820 --> 00:33:42,020
to admit that they're not machines,
that they're not superhuman.

538
00:33:42,220 --> 00:33:46,100
And that's so encouraging.
The same is true in tennis.

539
00:33:46,340 --> 00:33:48,500
Zverev has talked about it,
Ruud has as well,

540
00:33:48,740 --> 00:33:51,020
everyone is speaking openly
about this side of sport.

541
00:33:51,220 --> 00:33:53,660
I think that's a good thing
because the people

542
00:33:54,020 --> 00:33:57,220
who watch and follow sports
can relate to athletes.

543
00:33:57,420 --> 00:34:00,420
At the same time, though, they often see
these people as untouchable.

544
00:34:00,620 --> 00:34:03,700
But we have our own worries
and problems too,

545
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:08,620
regardless of the other side
of the equation, financial security,

546
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:11,700
success, medals, and everything else.

547
00:34:11,900 --> 00:34:16,260
There's also the human side,
and we all go through it.

548
00:34:16,940 --> 00:34:21,500
We need to talk about it
because it isn't easy.

549
00:34:22,860 --> 00:34:25,540
We also need to inspire
the next generation to understand

550
00:34:25,652 --> 00:34:27,372
that it's okay to struggle.

551
00:34:27,494 --> 00:34:33,107
But if you come back to yourself,
to your roots and your visions,

552
00:34:33,327 --> 00:34:35,955
somehow you find
a greater sense of peace.

553
00:34:36,020 --> 00:34:39,859
With many athletes, especially in sports
where there's a lot of money involved,

554
00:34:39,984 --> 00:34:42,422
it's often the parents who put pressure
on their children

555
00:34:42,630 --> 00:34:47,920
because they want them to succeed.
-Yes. The problem is that...

556
00:34:49,452 --> 00:34:51,693
The mind works in strange ways,
especially when it comes

557
00:34:51,863 --> 00:34:53,863
to public comments.

558
00:34:55,946 --> 00:35:00,810
Even if the overwhelming
majority of people are on your side,

559
00:35:01,041 --> 00:35:04,601
there are always enough
negative voices.

560
00:35:04,770 --> 00:35:08,130
And for some reason, that's
what the human brain remembers.

561
00:35:08,376 --> 00:35:12,376
They even say it can take
two hundred positive comments

562
00:35:12,529 --> 00:35:15,943
to cancel out the impact
of a single negative one.

563
00:35:16,780 --> 00:35:20,982
Or, as you mentioned earlier,
people will say,

564
00:35:21,047 --> 00:35:23,826
"For that kind of money,
I'd gladly put up with it."

565
00:35:24,394 --> 00:35:27,850
Of course, money matters,
we're not going to pretend otherwise.

566
00:35:28,013 --> 00:35:30,133
It makes a lot of things in life easier.

567
00:35:30,224 --> 00:35:32,928
But it doesn't make you
any less emotionally vulnerable.

568
00:35:33,142 --> 00:35:38,422
Absolutely. Absolutely.
And that's not what life is really about.

569
00:35:38,576 --> 00:35:41,029
Material things can make life
more comfortable.

570
00:35:41,136 --> 00:35:44,317
They can give you opportunities, like
traveling, which was the case for me.

571
00:35:45,645 --> 00:35:47,565
But the real issue goes much,
much deeper.

572
00:35:47,780 --> 00:35:53,260
The problem is that people
are becoming increasingly disconnected

573
00:35:53,429 --> 00:35:57,829
from who they really are. Instead,
they're chasing material satisfaction

574
00:35:57,977 --> 00:36:03,296
and trying to live up
to a certain image or lifestyle.

575
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:06,620
But isn't that something
that's being pushed on us?

576
00:36:06,820 --> 00:36:10,620
Of course it is.
-When I went to work in Russia...

577
00:36:11,900 --> 00:36:15,192
After the collapse of the Soviet system
and everything the country went through,

578
00:36:15,369 --> 00:36:17,765
society changed dramatically.

579
00:36:18,486 --> 00:36:26,832
Surveys showed that every second child,
and about eight out of ten boys,

580
00:36:27,055 --> 00:36:30,375
wanted to become some kind of gangster
because those were the people

581
00:36:30,474 --> 00:36:32,400
driving the nicest cars.

582
00:36:33,057 --> 00:36:36,257
And eight out of ten girls
wanted to become models.

583
00:36:36,411 --> 00:36:39,361
That's the image they were
constantly being sold. -Exactly.

584
00:36:39,490 --> 00:36:42,610
We're teaching children the wrong
values through the media.

585
00:36:42,849 --> 00:36:46,849
That's exactly what we were talking
about before we started recording.

586
00:36:46,937 --> 00:36:49,417
I think it's one of society's
biggest problems.

587
00:36:49,577 --> 00:36:51,923
Everything has become so polarized.

588
00:36:52,020 --> 00:36:54,900
If you're on one side, then you can't
possibly belong to the other.

589
00:36:55,076 --> 00:37:00,436
There's this constant pressure
to fit into a certain mold,

590
00:37:00,806 --> 00:37:06,220
and that's completely wrong.
One of my biggest dreams is

591
00:37:06,439 --> 00:37:09,915
to help people rediscover
the values that truly matter,

592
00:37:10,009 --> 00:37:12,642
to remind them what is
actually important in life.

593
00:37:12,848 --> 00:37:17,488
Because on Instagram it may look
as though someone has the perfect life.

594
00:37:17,698 --> 00:37:21,578
But what happens when they go home?
Do they spend time with their family?

595
00:37:21,654 --> 00:37:22,959
Do they laugh together?
Do they talk?

596
00:37:23,033 --> 00:37:25,021
Or do they just sit there,
staring at their screens?

597
00:37:25,141 --> 00:37:27,753
How many families do you see
sitting in restaurants,

598
00:37:27,818 --> 00:37:30,197
and looking at their phone?
To me, that's heartbreaking.

599
00:37:30,316 --> 00:37:31,912
People are losing real
human connection,

600
00:37:32,030 --> 00:37:34,166
real communication,
and that simple human element.

601
00:37:35,511 --> 00:37:39,176
If we keep going in this direction,
it definitely won't end well.

602
00:37:39,457 --> 00:37:42,465
But thankfully, more and more people
are waking up and realizing

603
00:37:42,591 --> 00:37:45,019
how much all of this
affects mental health.

604
00:37:45,114 --> 00:37:47,535
Absolutely. No question about it.

605
00:37:47,636 --> 00:37:51,847
And that brings us to something
you've said before,

606
00:37:51,944 --> 00:37:54,259
that the younger generations doen't have
the same work ethic anymore.

607
00:37:54,384 --> 00:37:56,024
Is that right? -Da.

608
00:37:56,217 --> 00:37:58,853
Growing up, nobody ever asked us
what we wanted.

609
00:37:58,961 --> 00:38:00,860
I remember the bombing, for example.

610
00:38:00,953 --> 00:38:03,500
I'd get up at six in the morning,
hearing the air raid sirens signaling

611
00:38:03,579 --> 00:38:05,711
the end of the air raid alert.

612
00:38:05,910 --> 00:38:09,574
Nobody asked me whether I wanted
to wake up at five-thirty, get ready,

613
00:38:09,729 --> 00:38:13,289
and go to practice during
those few hours when it was safe.

614
00:38:13,527 --> 00:38:16,487
Or when I was in school,
I started classes at eight,

615
00:38:16,624 --> 00:38:18,904
but I was already training
at five or six in the morning,

616
00:38:19,081 --> 00:38:22,041
in an inflatable clay-court bubble
where it was barely two degrees Celsius.

617
00:38:22,210 --> 00:38:26,250
Nobody asked if you felt like it.
Today, though, everything seems

618
00:38:26,410 --> 00:38:29,676
to revolve around questions like,
"What's my work-life balance?"

619
00:38:29,816 --> 00:38:33,793
But what does balance even mean?
-Exactly. Exactly.

620
00:38:37,100 --> 00:38:41,143
When we talk about elite athletes,
you've mentioned talent

621
00:38:41,189 --> 00:38:43,349
and you've mentioned hard work.

622
00:38:44,518 --> 00:38:49,518
What's the single
most important ingredient?

623
00:38:49,617 --> 00:38:54,432
Passion. Love for what you do.
Commitment. -Discipline. Commitment.

624
00:38:54,692 --> 00:38:56,732
Showing up to practice
even on the days

625
00:38:56,831 --> 00:39:01,338
when you don't feel like it.
Most days, you won't feel like going.

626
00:39:01,498 --> 00:39:03,658
I think most people would
rather stay in bed at five

627
00:39:03,811 --> 00:39:06,627
or six in the morning.
-Sleeping is definitely more appealing.

628
00:39:07,155 --> 00:39:09,354
Depends who you ask.

629
00:39:10,032 --> 00:39:12,792
That's the part people don't see.
That's what discipline really is.

630
00:39:12,899 --> 00:39:15,049
And it's not just about competing.

631
00:39:15,112 --> 00:39:18,752
It's years and years of sacrifice,
or, as I prefer to call it,

632
00:39:18,860 --> 00:39:20,579
years of making a choice.

633
00:39:20,725 --> 00:39:23,861
At any point, you can walk away
and choose a different life.

634
00:39:23,975 --> 00:39:26,643
But people think becoming
a professional athlete is easy.

635
00:39:26,702 --> 00:39:28,907
They think staying
at that level is easy, too.

636
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:32,780
You once said that people
only see the tip of the pyramid.

637
00:39:32,980 --> 00:39:34,940
That's something we've been
trying to show through this program

638
00:39:35,055 --> 00:39:37,384
as well, that it's not
as simple as it looks.

639
00:39:37,688 --> 00:39:39,688
It's difficult, but it is possible.

640
00:39:39,834 --> 00:39:42,314
I've heard people say so many times,
"It must be amazing.

641
00:39:42,412 --> 00:39:45,008
You travel the world and get to enjoy
the most beautiful cities."

642
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:48,197
It sounds amazing. But how often
do you actually get to see those cities?

643
00:39:48,281 --> 00:39:50,284
It's hotel, training club, hotel,
training club, airport...

644
00:39:50,442 --> 00:39:52,842
...and then you're gone.
-Most of the time, exactly.

645
00:39:53,860 --> 00:39:56,780
You also told me you're not interested
in becoming a coach.

646
00:39:57,020 --> 00:40:00,943
Given your intelligence
and everything you've experienced,

647
00:40:01,084 --> 00:40:04,295
you have so much to offer.
-Thank you. -Why not?

648
00:40:04,613 --> 00:40:10,420
For one thing, I wouldn't want to spend
my life traveling to tournaments again.

649
00:40:10,725 --> 00:40:13,185
Partly because of my family.

650
00:40:13,482 --> 00:40:17,420
But I would love to mentor someone.
I'm much more interested

651
00:40:17,566 --> 00:40:20,951
in the mental side of performance
because I know what I've been through.

652
00:40:21,240 --> 00:40:23,320
I've invested a great deal of time
working on myself

653
00:40:23,426 --> 00:40:26,514
and overcoming those challenges.
If I were still competing

654
00:40:26,620 --> 00:40:30,300
with everything I've learned,
especially over the last ten years,

655
00:40:30,740 --> 00:40:33,220
I would handle many situations
so much better.

656
00:40:33,420 --> 00:40:36,060
That's the knowledge I'd like to
pass on to someone else.

657
00:40:36,580 --> 00:40:41,980
But my biggest goal is actually to work
with teenagers and children.

658
00:40:42,220 --> 00:40:45,633
I want to help them understand
what real values are in life.

659
00:40:45,899 --> 00:40:50,469
So many young people,
especially during those formative years

660
00:40:50,629 --> 00:40:52,709
when they're deciding
whether to go to university,

661
00:40:52,893 --> 00:40:55,414
pursue a career in sports,
or follow another path,

662
00:40:55,549 --> 00:40:58,309
end up focusing on what their
friends expect of them,

663
00:40:58,399 --> 00:41:00,319
or what their parents expect of them.

664
00:41:00,424 --> 00:41:02,424
I want them to have the courage
to follow their own path.

665
00:41:02,522 --> 00:41:04,684
Because deep down,
we all know what our own path is.

666
00:41:04,805 --> 00:41:07,411
Sometimes we just don't want to listen
to that inner voice.

667
00:41:08,065 --> 00:41:12,580
But if we could find more peace,
we'd all know the answer.

668
00:41:12,714 --> 00:41:15,290
Deep down, we all know
which path is the right one.

669
00:41:15,412 --> 00:41:18,550
The problem is that so many children
and teenagers end up choosing

670
00:41:18,684 --> 00:41:23,055
the wrong path because of peer pressure,
because of social media....

671
00:41:23,185 --> 00:41:26,121
And where does that lead?
Years later, you find yourself

672
00:41:26,271 --> 00:41:30,879
talking to someone
who's struggling with panic attacks.

673
00:41:31,153 --> 00:41:33,993
That's true. It really is.

674
00:41:36,140 --> 00:41:39,075
So, tennis is obviously your sport...

675
00:41:42,140 --> 00:41:44,839
Have you ever been drawn
to any other sport?

676
00:41:45,500 --> 00:41:48,300
Is there one you especially like?

677
00:41:48,700 --> 00:41:53,020
When I was younger, I loved basketball.
And I really liked volleyball too.

678
00:41:53,220 --> 00:41:55,220
But I never actually
trained either of them.

679
00:41:56,180 --> 00:42:01,292
Do you think you could have
adapted to a team sport?

680
00:42:01,464 --> 00:42:03,555
In a team, you have to
rely on other people

681
00:42:03,675 --> 00:42:05,839
because everything
isn't entirely in your hands.

682
00:42:05,995 --> 00:42:08,187
Do you think you would
have been successful?

683
00:42:08,338 --> 00:42:12,097
I'm not sure. I never trained
or really tried any team sports

684
00:42:12,230 --> 00:42:15,237
because I started playing tennis
at such a young age.

685
00:42:15,351 --> 00:42:18,011
To be honest, even in tennis
I never liked playing doubles.

686
00:42:18,392 --> 00:42:23,855
I never really felt comfortable with it.
If I made a mistake

687
00:42:24,040 --> 00:42:28,675
and we lost a point because of me,
I'd immediately feel guilty. -Really?

688
00:42:29,466 --> 00:42:34,089
Yes. That's why an individual sport
suited me perfectly.

689
00:42:34,251 --> 00:42:36,131
I like taking responsibility.

690
00:42:36,236 --> 00:42:38,396
I like knowing that
the outcome depends on me.

691
00:42:38,533 --> 00:42:42,210
If I did something well,
or if I made a mistake, that was on me.

692
00:42:42,337 --> 00:42:43,655
That's interesting.

693
00:42:43,815 --> 00:42:45,749
You're someone who likes
taking responsibility.

694
00:42:45,862 --> 00:42:48,921
The problem is... you tend to take
all the responsibility.

695
00:42:49,895 --> 00:42:51,841
And that's a little complicated.

696
00:42:51,981 --> 00:42:54,701
My coaches and hitting partners
used to tease me.

697
00:42:54,815 --> 00:42:58,114
Even when they made a mistake
during our warm-up rallies,

698
00:42:58,339 --> 00:43:00,019
I'd still say, "Sorry."

699
00:43:04,340 --> 00:43:07,457
Let's switch gears for a moment
and talk about your personal life.

700
00:43:07,607 --> 00:43:11,410
You have three sons, from your marriage
to a former elite athlete, right?

701
00:43:11,549 --> 00:43:13,886
Although he comes from a team sport.

702
00:43:14,074 --> 00:43:17,261
Have you ever noticed a difference
in personality between someone

703
00:43:17,376 --> 00:43:19,656
who's played a team sport
and someone like you,

704
00:43:19,746 --> 00:43:21,479
who comes from an individual sport?

705
00:43:21,593 --> 00:43:24,233
It actually ties in perfectly
with what we've been talking about.

706
00:43:24,355 --> 00:43:28,355
As an individual athlete, you're
used to taking so much on yourself

707
00:43:28,464 --> 00:43:33,075
that it just feels normal.
By the end of my career,

708
00:43:33,106 --> 00:43:36,346
I was the one arranging everything
for my coaches, booking the hotels,

709
00:43:36,500 --> 00:43:39,368
organizing the flights, meals...
I took care of all of it.

710
00:43:39,447 --> 00:43:40,915
In a team sport, it's different.

711
00:43:41,008 --> 00:43:42,848
You show up at a certain time,
someone brings you

712
00:43:42,893 --> 00:43:44,350
a coffee and everything.

713
00:43:44,468 --> 00:43:50,388
Even at the very highest level...
...for both you and your husband?

714
00:43:51,980 --> 00:43:55,740
We touched on this earlier.
How are you raising your children?

715
00:43:55,966 --> 00:44:02,166
On one hand, they'll always carry
the weight of the family name.

716
00:44:02,470 --> 00:44:04,994
But on the other, they also have
the security and comfort

717
00:44:05,120 --> 00:44:07,283
that comes with growing up
in your circumstances. -Exactly.

718
00:44:07,534 --> 00:44:13,721
I approached this in a way that
I never told them what I used to do,

719
00:44:14,179 --> 00:44:15,862
nor did I burden them with it.

720
00:44:15,970 --> 00:44:19,252
Of course, they know now,
and so do their classmates.

721
00:44:19,693 --> 00:44:23,614
But I have never went
into detail.

722
00:44:24,034 --> 00:44:26,362
I believe they are still young
and there is no need for them

723
00:44:26,441 --> 00:44:28,230
to be burdened by any of it.

724
00:44:28,291 --> 00:44:30,322
Nor do I want them to
have any expectations.

725
00:44:30,442 --> 00:44:33,500
I'm their mother. I want them
to have the freedom to choose...

726
00:44:33,590 --> 00:44:35,931
who they want to become in life.

727
00:44:35,989 --> 00:44:40,606
Of course, I'm here to guide them
towards the right values

728
00:44:40,751 --> 00:44:43,512
and to give them everything I can.

729
00:44:43,875 --> 00:44:47,039
In the end, whatever path they choose,
whether they decide to pursue sport

730
00:44:47,183 --> 00:44:49,703
or not, that will be their own decision.

731
00:44:49,965 --> 00:44:56,925
I believe, that if I raise them with
the right values, it won't matter to them

732
00:44:57,140 --> 00:45:00,660
who their parents are or what they do,
because they will have their own calling.

733
00:45:00,750 --> 00:45:02,670
Maybe they'll reach a higher
or lower level,

734
00:45:02,807 --> 00:45:05,327
maybe they'll choose a completely
different profession.

735
00:45:05,481 --> 00:45:08,848
But if they believe in themselves,
in their values, and in their own path,

736
00:45:08,974 --> 00:45:12,134
then nothing from the outside
can affect them.

737
00:45:12,660 --> 00:45:15,900
That's my philosophy and
my approach to parenting.

738
00:45:17,220 --> 00:45:21,020
What about sport? Do you recommend it?
-Yes. -To what extent?

739
00:45:22,060 --> 00:45:25,700
Unfortunately, they mostly play
football because they're boys,

740
00:45:25,940 --> 00:45:30,580
but they also enjoy taekwondo. They've
recently started playing tennis as well.

741
00:45:30,820 --> 00:45:35,260
They love skiing and swimming.
They're typical boys.

742
00:45:35,405 --> 00:45:38,685
Sport is part of our everyday life,
and an important part of our home.

743
00:45:38,900 --> 00:45:40,380
But to what extent?

744
00:45:40,620 --> 00:45:46,555
Would you encourage them to reach
the very top, like you did?

745
00:45:46,741 --> 00:45:49,301
On the other hand...
-I know what that means.

746
00:45:49,437 --> 00:45:51,397
You know everything that comes with it.

747
00:45:51,489 --> 00:45:53,363
Sport today is becoming
increasingly ruthless.

748
00:45:53,526 --> 00:45:56,496
It's not getting any easier, it's becoming
more demanding and more extreme.

749
00:45:56,671 --> 00:45:59,288
It's becoming less sport
and more about business.

750
00:45:59,761 --> 00:46:03,589
In many ways, it has lost
part of its essence, but it's still sport.

751
00:46:03,704 --> 00:46:06,305
And there's nothing better than sport.
-Exactly.

752
00:46:07,660 --> 00:46:11,220
As far as I'm concerned, I'll say
it again, the choice is theirs.

753
00:46:11,334 --> 00:46:13,574
Personally, I'd probably encourage
a team sport,

754
00:46:13,821 --> 00:46:19,061
because I think it's mentally easier
than an individual sport.

755
00:46:19,540 --> 00:46:22,140
But I'm not the one
to make that decision.

756
00:46:22,340 --> 00:46:24,820
I simply give them the opportunity
to choose.

757
00:46:25,020 --> 00:46:29,220
If they want to play football,
as they do now, play matches,

758
00:46:29,420 --> 00:46:31,820
then I'll fully support them.

759
00:46:32,100 --> 00:46:35,780
If that's their choice, I'll give them
the best support I possibly can.

760
00:46:36,420 --> 00:46:39,060
But they have to prove themselves.

761
00:46:39,562 --> 00:46:45,570
Zlatan Ibrahimoviæ said it perfectly,
when he was my guest before you...

762
00:46:45,888 --> 00:46:48,328
He also has two sons.

763
00:46:49,820 --> 00:46:53,220
Team sports and individual sports
are two completely different things.

764
00:46:53,460 --> 00:47:00,420
In a team sport, you experience team spirit
and the importance of the collective.

765
00:47:00,541 --> 00:47:04,152
But even in a team sport, you still
have to stand out as an individual.

766
00:47:04,250 --> 00:47:05,370
That's right.

767
00:47:05,430 --> 00:47:08,458
That was a great point
and I've always remembered it.

768
00:47:08,940 --> 00:47:17,067
If I had had the mindset of one of my sons,
I probably would have achieved even more.

769
00:47:17,391 --> 00:47:20,778
He's so relaxed about everything.

770
00:47:22,380 --> 00:47:26,107
He jokes around and has that confidence.

771
00:47:26,213 --> 00:47:31,169
Whereas I was much more reserved and
put far too much pressure on myself.

772
00:47:31,314 --> 00:47:33,888
Too much pressure? -Yes.

773
00:47:37,300 --> 00:47:40,860
When you arrived and I asked you
about Palma de Mallorca,

774
00:47:41,100 --> 00:47:49,420
you said you like living here because,
when it comes to social media

775
00:47:49,740 --> 00:47:55,300
and technology,
the environment is less extreme.

776
00:47:55,540 --> 00:47:58,420
That it's a good place
to raise children.

777
00:47:58,660 --> 00:48:01,220
How do you protect them from
this plague social media,

778
00:48:01,420 --> 00:48:03,860
mobile phones and PlayStation?

779
00:48:04,300 --> 00:48:09,580
I'm against all of that, because I know
how harmful it can be for children.

780
00:48:09,820 --> 00:48:15,020
They're still young. They don't have
an iPad, a mobile phone or a smartwatch.

781
00:48:15,820 --> 00:48:20,140
They're only allowed to use those things
at weekends, for 20 to 30 minutes.

782
00:48:20,300 --> 00:48:26,540
Because they love sport, I let them play
a sports video game from time to time,

783
00:48:26,654 --> 00:48:29,180
but not on a PlayStation,
only on my phone.

784
00:48:29,421 --> 00:48:32,701
I allow it because of their friends, but
overall I'm completely against all of it.

785
00:48:32,980 --> 00:48:37,580
I don't think they need it. The weather
here is beautiful all year round.

786
00:48:37,820 --> 00:48:42,180
They can play outside or at home.
We have lots of board games.

787
00:48:42,380 --> 00:48:46,220
It takes more effort, because I have to
be much more involved, but I enjoy it.

788
00:48:46,420 --> 00:48:49,740
I enjoy spending that time with them.

789
00:48:49,980 --> 00:48:54,980
I believe those are the bonds you
build for a lifetime.

790
00:48:55,380 --> 00:49:00,220
When they become teenagers,
they'll have their own friends.

791
00:49:00,620 --> 00:49:05,500
Do they complain? -No.
They don't ask for it or demand it.

792
00:49:05,740 --> 00:49:09,660
That's good. The key is your consistency.

793
00:49:10,900 --> 00:49:17,140
It's interesting that you said, even other
11-year-olds don't have those devices?

794
00:49:17,340 --> 00:49:20,020
So the environment has an influence,
doesn't it?

795
00:49:20,220 --> 00:49:22,740
If you lived in New York, that would
probably be much harder. -Absolutely!

796
00:49:22,940 --> 00:49:26,808
It helps that we live on an island
where the weather is always nice.

797
00:49:26,939 --> 00:49:29,385
In general, children can spend
much more time outdoors.

798
00:49:29,529 --> 00:49:32,533
We're not living in a big city where
they can't simply go outside or walk

799
00:49:32,588 --> 00:49:35,244
to a park, so children end up spending
all their time indoors.

800
00:49:35,376 --> 00:49:37,471
Most of their friends live the same way.

801
00:49:37,683 --> 00:49:41,682
Their classmates have older brothers.

802
00:49:42,126 --> 00:49:44,606
He is 13 and still doesn't have
a smartwatch?

803
00:49:44,775 --> 00:49:50,175
So they're not attached to technology.
Most parents here share my opinion.

804
00:49:50,390 --> 00:49:53,235
Of course, some are stricter
and others are more relaxed.

805
00:49:53,350 --> 00:49:55,618
But I don't think children
need those things.

806
00:49:55,758 --> 00:49:57,478
I put a lot of emphasis
on how I was raised.

807
00:49:57,684 --> 00:50:00,879
I grew up in a family where lunch
and dinner happened at set times.

808
00:50:00,999 --> 00:50:03,575
Family meals were important.

809
00:50:03,716 --> 00:50:08,163
The television stayed off, no one used
a phone, we simply talked to each other.

810
00:50:08,420 --> 00:50:11,484
That's exactly what I do with my children.

811
00:50:11,765 --> 00:50:14,562
To them, it's completely normal.

812
00:50:14,773 --> 00:50:16,493
There's a funny story...

813
00:50:16,783 --> 00:50:19,719
When they were three or four years
old and they were ill,

814
00:50:19,874 --> 00:50:22,114
I would sometimes put on children's songs

815
00:50:22,237 --> 00:50:24,648
because it's hard for a child
to lie in bed all day.

816
00:50:24,871 --> 00:50:32,511
One day my oldest son asked my mum,
"Grandma, can I watch the wall?"

817
00:50:32,595 --> 00:50:34,155
She said, "Excuse me?"

818
00:50:34,393 --> 00:50:36,073
"Can I watch the wall?"

819
00:50:36,484 --> 00:50:38,084
"What wall?"

820
00:50:38,331 --> 00:50:40,891
"You know... the thing on the wall."

821
00:50:41,050 --> 00:50:43,610
He didn't even know what
a television was called.

822
00:50:44,264 --> 00:50:47,744
That's wonderful, but you also
said it's not easy.

823
00:50:49,940 --> 00:50:53,886
Once again, it comes back to education
and values, because parents sometimes

824
00:50:53,982 --> 00:50:56,069
choose the easier option.
I'm not judging anyone.

825
00:50:56,190 --> 00:50:58,921
Sometimes they simply don't have
a choice. -Exactly.

826
00:50:59,020 --> 00:51:00,866
Sometimes there is no choice.

827
00:51:01,016 --> 00:51:06,827
I was fortunate in my career, because I was
able to devote my time to my children.

828
00:51:07,119 --> 00:51:10,039
I never had to rely on those
kinds of distractions,

829
00:51:10,240 --> 00:51:12,080
whereas some parents do.

830
00:51:12,351 --> 00:51:15,711
I don't want to judge anyone,
everyone has their own circumstances

831
00:51:15,935 --> 00:51:19,455
and their own choices, but I believe
that, in general, less is better.

832
00:51:19,820 --> 00:51:24,500
Some people have to go to work,
and others judge them when

833
00:51:24,700 --> 00:51:31,620
they go to a restaurant, hand their child
a phone, and enjoy a peaceful meal.

834
00:51:31,860 --> 00:51:35,500
But what about the other twenty-three
and a half hours of the day? -Exactly.

835
00:51:35,716 --> 00:51:38,067
That's a very good point, Ana.
-Well, I do it sometimes too.

836
00:51:38,205 --> 00:51:42,715
Right now, during the World Cup,
they want to see the scores,

837
00:51:43,085 --> 00:51:45,685
so they ask to check them on my phone.

838
00:51:45,847 --> 00:51:49,287
They don't watch the highlights.
They just look up the match results.

839
00:51:49,401 --> 00:51:52,371
They already know who's playing
and when.

840
00:51:52,461 --> 00:51:54,661
So they're real football fans!

841
00:51:56,020 --> 00:51:59,940
Ana, before we finish,
do you have a message

842
00:52:00,008 --> 00:52:02,648
for children, mothers and parents?

843
00:52:02,919 --> 00:52:07,999
When it comes to parents, they should
really listen to their children.

844
00:52:08,182 --> 00:52:10,542
Children tell us everything.

845
00:52:10,695 --> 00:52:14,895
Take their wishes and their choices
seriously.

846
00:52:15,018 --> 00:52:18,018
Many parents project their
own ambitions

847
00:52:18,233 --> 00:52:20,545
and unfulfilled dreams onto
their children.

848
00:52:20,656 --> 00:52:23,124
That should be put aside.
Listen to your children.

849
00:52:23,222 --> 00:52:28,524
As for children, they should stay
true to themselves, their values

850
00:52:28,704 --> 00:52:32,076
and their own path.
They shouldn't give up.

851
00:52:32,344 --> 00:52:38,015
Nowadays many children and young
people quit too easily.

852
00:52:38,275 --> 00:52:41,960
They try one sport, then another,
and eventually lose their focus.

853
00:52:42,263 --> 00:52:45,263
Choose your path
and stay committed to it.

854
00:52:45,432 --> 00:52:47,432
Every path has obstacles.

855
00:52:47,554 --> 00:52:52,506
Success requires hard work and
discipline, we've talked about that.

856
00:52:52,718 --> 00:53:00,249
That's the only road to success
and personal fulfilment.

857
00:53:00,722 --> 00:53:03,140
You're absolutely right,
when you said it's important

858
00:53:03,283 --> 00:53:05,435
to find a balance
and give children freedom.

859
00:53:05,540 --> 00:53:08,022
But when they feel like giving up,
that's when they need

860
00:53:08,153 --> 00:53:10,208
their parents' unconditional support.

861
00:53:10,342 --> 00:53:13,638
Parents should encourage them to keep
going just a little longer. -Exactly.

862
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:16,505
Absolutely. Thank you, Ana.
-Thank you.

863
00:53:17,060 --> 00:53:18,340

